  "clues": {
    "kinetics": "Nearly all of an oral dose is converted to the active form. A meal lowers peak concentration by 48%, protein binding is 92.9% to 93.9%, and feces carry 80.1% of the dose.",
    "substance": "The molecular mass is 571.55 grams per mole, and it is practically insoluble in water.",
    "product": "Approved under an NDA as 40 mg and 80 mg tablets, and as 30 mg and 40 mg granule packets; a single oral dose set by body weight.",
    "safety": "No boxed warning. Contraindicated after any prior hypersensitivity to the drug or its ingredients; anaphylaxis and angioedema are among the serious allergic reactions.",
    "indication": "Treatment of acute uncomplicated influenza in patients 5 years and older symptomatic for no more than 48 hours, and post-exposure prophylaxis of influenza in patients 5 years and older.",
    "mechanism": "It inhibits the influenza virus polymerase acidic (PA) endonuclease, an enzyme the virus needs, and acts as an antiviral against influenza virus."
  },
  "tell": "Its 79-hour terminal half-life, and a meal that cuts peak concentration by almost half, set this PA endonuclease inhibitor apart.",
